  var CUES = [
    // ── SCENE 1 · COLD OPEN (0–70) ──
    [1, 5, "Somewhere on a server sit 493 clips from one conference."],
    [6.5, 3.5, "Nobody will ever watch them."],
    [10.5, 5.5, "Not because they are bad. Editing one hour of interview takes 40 to 60 hours of human work."],
    [16.5, 4.5, "Only one in five corporate videos ships on time."],
    [21.5, 5.5, "And video is 82% of all internet traffic. The content already exists."],
    [27.5, 5, "The bottleneck is the edit: the human hours between filming and publishing."],
    [33, 7, "This is the story of a machine that removes that bottleneck. An AI film crew, built from rented parts, that turns one upload into three finished cuts for 34 cents."],
    [41, 3.5, "But this is not really a story about video."],
    [45.5, 6, "Under this system, and under every AI system you have ever used, sits the same hidden structure."],
    [52.5, 6, "Five layers. Learn to see them, and you can read any AI system like a map."],
    [60, 4.5, "So grab a hard hat. We are going down."],
    // ── SCENE 2 · THESIS / OSI (70–150) ──
    [76, 6.5, "First, a quick history lesson. In 1984 the internet was becoming too complex for any one person to hold in their head."],
    [83, 6.5, "The fix was to cut it into seven layers. Each layer does one job and talks only to its neighbors. Engineers called it the OSI model."],
    [90, 6.5, "That trick is called layering, and it is why you can use Wi-Fi without ever thinking about copper wires. Forty years later, it still organizes how engineers think."],
    [97.5, 5, "Modern AI systems have quietly grown the same kind of anatomy. Five layers instead of seven."],
    [103, 9.5, "Interface: what you click. Orchestration: what gives orders. Compute: what does the work. AI runtime: what makes judgment calls. State: where everything rests."],
    [113.5, 6.5, "Why should you care? Because once you can name the layers, AI systems stop being magic. Every diagram, every tutorial, every job ad suddenly has a place to hang."],
    [120.5, 7, "To prove it, we drill through all five using one real production system: VideoFlow, a pipeline on AWS that edits raw footage into finished videos. No humans in the loop."],
    [128, 7, "Built by Linda Mohamed, an AWS Hero from Vienna. Every box you are about to see is a real service, in a real account, with a real bill."],
    [136.5, 4.5, "The full beginner course is free at ai-solutions.wiki. Five layers. One upload. Time to go down."],
    // ── SCENE 3 · LAYER 1: INTERFACE (150–235) ──
    [152.5, 4.5, "Layer one: the interface. The only layer most people ever see."],
    [158, 7, "For VideoFlow it is a web dashboard, a Next.js app, where an operator drags in footage and watches the pipeline run."],
    [166, 7, "But a click is just the surface. Underneath, every button becomes a request to a REST API. Here that is FastAPI, with every route documented."],
    [174, 7.5, "In front of it, CloudFront caches the app close to you and handles encryption. API Gateway checks who you are before a single function runs."],
    [182.5, 6.5, "Notice what the interface does not do. It makes no decisions. It owns no data. It is a thin pane of glass over everything below."],
    [190, 5.5, "That is the discipline of a layered system. The layer you touch is deliberately the dumbest one."],
    [197, 7, "The dashboard, the searchable media library, the use-case wizard: all of it just reads and writes through that one API."],
    [215, 7, "And when the operator drops a file, the interface does exactly one thing. It puts the video in a bucket, and walks away."],
    [224, 4, "Who picks it up? That is layer two."],
    // ── SCENE 4 · LAYER 2: ORCHESTRATION (235–330) ──
    [237.5, 4, "Layer two: orchestration. The layer that gives orders."],
    [242.5, 7, "The moment a file lands in storage, an event fires, and a Step Functions workflow wakes up. Think of it as a recipe the computer follows, step by step."],
    [250.5, 6.5, "It lists every step, runs them in order, retries what fails, and shows exactly where something broke."],
    [258, 7, "VideoFlow runs nine of these workflows. The main one drives the whole pipeline, and it never does any work itself. It only delegates."],
    [266.5, 8, "Here is the pattern that makes it cheap. To wait on a slow job, the workflow asks: are you done? If not, it pauses. Completely."],
    [275.5, 7.5, "Nothing runs during a Wait state. Polling a transcription job costs zero compute. One pattern, reused for every slow job in the system."],
    [284, 8, "Orchestration is also where time lives. EventBridge fires the schedules, queues decouple the work, alarms watch the metrics."],
    [293, 8, "And when something fails at 2 a.m., the execution history shows every state with its input, its output, and the exact error, inline. No log hunting."],
    [302, 6, "An assembly line where every station is visible, and the conveyor belt itself is free."],
    [322, 4.5, "But orders are nothing without workers. Layer three."],
    // ── SCENE 5 · LAYER 3: COMPUTE (330–415) ──
    [332.5, 4, "Layer three: compute. The layer that does the work."],
    [337.5, 7, "VideoFlow has no servers. It has about 38 Lambda functions: small pieces of code that exist only while they are needed."],
    [345.5, 6, "A Lambda is a motion-sensor light. It switches on when something happens, runs for seconds, then disappears."],
    [352.5, 6, "One checks the file. One extracts audio. One builds a clip. Each does a single job, then stops existing."],
    [359.5, 8, "For the heavy lifting there is a different shape. One MediaConvert job takes the upload and emits three derivatives at once."],
    [368.5, 7.5, "A small 540p proxy for editing. Clean audio for transcription. A frame every two seconds for vision. The original is never touched again."],
    [377, 7, "Containers on Fargate handle renders too big for a function. Batch bursts through frame jobs. Right tool, right size."],
    [385, 8, "Because compute only exists while running, an idle pipeline costs almost nothing. Ten thousand videos a month or zero: the workers clock out either way."],
    [404, 6, "But workers do not decide what to make. For that, this system has something stranger. Layer four."],
    // ── SCENE 6 · LAYER 4: AI RUNTIME (415–545) ──
    [417.5, 4, "Layer four: the AI runtime. The layer with judgment."],
    [422.5, 6.5, "Before anything can be decided, the system has to perceive. It watches the footage three ways at once: three senses, in parallel."],
    [430, 6.5, "Rekognition looks at the frames and labels what it sees: a visual timeline of the footage."],
    [437.5, 6.5, "Transcribe listens, writing down every word with its timestamp. What was said, and exactly when."],
    [445, 6.5, "And Bedrock Data Automation watches the whole video for meaning: the narrative summary a human would give you."],
    [452.5, 6, "Run one after another, that is three minutes of waiting. In parallel, all three finish in under ninety seconds."],
    [459.5, 9, "The three streams merge into one brief: title, summary, key moments. A foundation model drafts it, and the pipeline validates it as strict JSON. If the model returns garbage, the pipeline fails loudly. No fallback data. Ever."],
    [470, 5, "Then the brief goes to the strangest part of this machine: the film crew."],
    [476, 6, "Eight AI agents, each with one specialty, deliberate over your footage like a real production team."],
    [483, 9, "A Director owns the narrative arc. An Editor selects clips. A Cinematographer judges framing and light. A Sound Designer handles audio and sync."],
    [493, 9, "A Story Analyst finds the beats. A Pacing Analyst sets the rhythm of the cuts. A Fact Checker compares every claim against the analysis and flags what does not match."],
    [503, 6.5, "And last: a Quality Checker. The final gate. It can reject the whole plan and send the crew back to work."],
    [510.5, 7, "They argue in tokens instead of around a table, with shared working memory, and they hand back one artifact: a VariantSpec."],
    [518.5, 7, "Which clips. In what order. With which transitions. For three aspect ratios: widescreen, vertical, square."],
    [526.5, 8.5, "Why eight agents instead of one big prompt? Because specialists check each other. A single model confidently makes things up. A crew with a fact checker gets caught."],
    [537, 5, "One layer left: the one everything else stands on."],
    // ── SCENE 7 · LAYER 5: STATE (545–625) ──
    [547.5, 3.5, "Layer five: state. The bedrock."],
    [552, 7, "Every layer above is allowed to vanish. Functions disappear, workflows end, agents forget. This layer is not."],
    [560, 7, "Three S3 buckets are the source of truth: input, output, assets. Everything else can be rebuilt from them."],
    [568, 6.5, "DynamoDB holds the metadata: every video, every job, the media library index. Answers in milliseconds."],
    [575.5, 7, "OpenSearch makes it findable, with semantic search over transcripts and embeddings. Ask for the part about pricing, and it knows."],
    [583.5, 8, "ElastiCache keeps hot reads instant. Aurora keeps the records where transactions matter. Shared file systems give render workers scratch space."],
    [592.5, 5.5, "Five kinds of memory, chosen by access pattern. Not one database asked to be everything."],
    [599.5, 8, "And notice: state is the only layer with gravity. Compute can be re-rented by the second. Your data cannot."],
    [615, 5, "That is the bottom of the dig. Now watch what the whole stack does together."],
    // ── SCENE 8 · FULL RUN (625–700) ──
    [627, 3.5, "One upload. Five layers. Real time."],
    [631, 8.5, "The interface drops the file in a bucket. Orchestration wakes. A sanitizer checks codec, container, duration, and whether this exact file was already processed. Never pay twice."],
    [640.5, 7, "Compute fans out: proxy, audio, frames. Then three senses in parallel: labels, transcript, meaning. Under ninety seconds."],
    [648.5, 7, "The brief is drafted and validated, the crew of eight deliberates, and a VariantSpec comes back."],
    [656.5, 7.5, "The compiler renders all three formats in a single MediaConvert job, and state receives the finished cuts."],
    [665, 6.5, "Under three minutes, end to end. Three videos: YouTube, Shorts, LinkedIn."],
    [673, 9.5, "And six validation gates stood guard the whole way: input, clip, timestamp, facts, quality, schema. Any one of them can stop the line. Confidently wrong output never reaches a viewer."],
    [684, 13, "A failed check halts the pipeline loudly. It never invents synthetic content to keep going."],
    [697, 2.5, "And the receipt for all of that?"],
    // ── SCENE 9 · CHEAP, FAST, CORRECT (700–780) ──
    [705, 5.5, "Thirty-four cents. Per finished video. Three formats included."],
    [711.5, 7, "At ten thousand videos a month, the entire pipeline bills about 3,400 euros. Less than one human editor."],
    [719.5, 6.5, "Not by replacing the editor\u2019s craft, but by handling the eighty percent of footage no editor would ever reach."],
    [727, 8.5, "The speed never came from faster services. It came from the shape: parallel branches, zero-compute waits, warm starts where cold starts hurt."],
    [736.5, 7.5, "And the safety came from layers that distrust each other. Validation at every boundary, workflows that fail loudly, agents that check agents."],
    [745, 6, "Cheap, fast, and correct are not features you add at the end. They fall out of the architecture."],
    [770, 7, "Which leaves one question. How does a system like this actually get built, and could you build one?"],
    // ── SCENE 10 · SHIP IT / SDLC (780–860) ──
    [782, 7, "Here is the part that sounded like science fiction five years ago. The whole platform is written as code, and a free guide walks you through building one."],
    [790, 8, "Everything you have seen, 38 functions, 9 workflows, the buckets, the roles, is written down as code. Terraform reads it and builds the cloud to match."],
    [799, 6.5, "The software process is a loop, not a line. Describe, generate, review, deploy, observe, and around again."],
    [806.5, 7.5, "On every push, GitHub Actions assumes a role with OIDC, so there are no long-lived keys. It plans the change, then applies it."],
    [815, 8, "Tests run against real AWS services, not mocks, for five cents a run. The mocks passed while production failed. Real tests are the only ones that count here."],
    [824, 9.5, "And changes? You describe them in plain English: add a square version for Instagram, title in the corner. The AI writes the diff. You read it. You ship it. That is vibecoding."],
    [835, 7.5, "The five layers are not just how the system runs. They are how it is built, tested, and changed, by one person, with a laptop."],
    [851, 3.5, "So step back up to the surface."],
    // ── SCENE 11 · RECAP (860–906) ──
    [861.5, 3.5, "Five layers, under every AI system."],
    [865.5, 7.5, "Interface: keep it thin. Orchestration: make every step visible. Compute: rent it by the second."],
    [873.5, 6.5, "AI runtime: let specialists check each other. State: choose memory by access pattern, and respect its gravity."],
    [881, 7.5, "Swap the services, and the same shape fits podcasts, photo libraries, documents, logs. The shape stays. The providers change."],
    [889.5, 8.5, "Footage that would never be published now gets published. That is the value. The architecture just makes it cheap, fast, and correct enough to trust."],
    // ── SCENE 12 · ENDCARD (906–930) ──
    [908, 5, "Built by Linda Mohamed, AWS Hero, Vienna."],
    [914, 5, "The full beginner course and build guide are free at ai-solutions.wiki. The blog, and my workshops, are at lindamohamed.com."]
  ];
